      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The question builds Nino 3.4 anomalies against a 1991-2020 monthly climatology, compares two baseline periods, and detects ONI events; this fragment defines baseline-relative anomalies, baseline offsets, and event persistence on anomalies.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Build RECON_9120 by removing the field's **own** monthly climatology computed from the 1991-2020 period (each calendar month matched to its own climatological month, which removes the seasonal cycle); rebuild RECON_8110 the same way against 1981-2010 and record climatology and `base_period` in the result attrs — an anomaly is defined only relative to the period its climatology came from, and two series on different baselines differ by one constant per calendar month, so the offset is not an error.",
        "Define events operationally by persistence, not by eye: a warm/cold phase counts only when the series stays beyond the threshold for a minimum number of consecutive steps (`min_duration` of `detect_threshold_events`); a single step beyond the threshold is noise. State which climatology and baseline each series (RECON, CPC 1991-2020, CPC 1981-2010, `oni`) was built on, since the CPC products differ in baseline and are not interchangeable."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "common-comparison-mask",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/common-comparison-mask.md",
      "sha256": "bbb98200f52b4a692ce097704b8c438179a795a44bec6b856c03cf31931af4ea",
      "reason": "The requested correlation, RMSE and largest deviation compare RECON_9120 with CPC, so the comparison must be made only over months where both products have data rather than assuming identical coverage.",
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "not_applicable_reason": "the comparison is between two monthly index time series (ERSSTv5-derived and CPC), with no regridding, multi-source cell-by-cell metric or land-sea comparison mask involved."
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "RECON_9120 is a regional surface mean over 5S-5N, 170W-120W, and this fragment constrains how surface means should be weighted over the grid.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The Niño 3.4 region average over the native 2° grid must be area-weighted, not a plain `mean()`: on a regular grid unweighted means over latitude are wrong because cells shrink towards the poles — use `compute_basic_stat` with `op='area_weighted_mean'` (region mask applied on the field's own grid, weights restricted to the cells with valid data).",
        "Every statistic over a map (not just means) needs area weighting: an unweighted RMSE, bias or pattern correlation counts a polar cell as heavily as a tropical one; `compute_difference_metrics` weights by area by default and `analyze_correlation` needs `weighting='area'`. The offset averages the question asks for are unweighted over time (one value per month), which is a different operation from the spatial weighting above."
      ]
    }
